## Proof

We prove the contrapositive-free form directly by contradiction. Assume, towards a contradiction, that **no** triple $x,y,z$ (pairwise distinct) satisfies $c_{xy}=c_{xz}\neq c_{yz}$. Equivalently, for every pairwise-distinct $x,y,z$,

$$c_{xy}=c_{xz}\ \Longrightarrow\ c_{yz}=c_{xy}=c_{xz}. \tag{$\ast$}$$

Fix an arbitrary vertex $a$. Since $a$ is incident with only finitely many colours, let $\alpha_1,\dots,\alpha_k$ be the distinct colours appearing on edges incident with $a$ ($k$ finite). Partition

$$V\setminus\{a\}=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k} S_i,\qquad S_i:=\{\,y\in V\setminus\{a\}:c_{ay}=\alpha_i\,\}.$$

**Claim 1.** For each $i$, the set $S_i\cup\{a\}$ is a monochromatic clique of colour $\alpha_i$ (every edge inside has colour $\alpha_i$).

*Proof.* Edges from $a$ to $S_i$ have colour $\alpha_i$ by definition. For $y,z\in S_i$ distinct, $c_{ay}=c_{az}=\alpha_i$, so by $(\ast)$ applied at $a$ we get $c_{yz}=\alpha_i$. ∎

**Claim 2.** Under the global hypothesis (two edges of different colours exist), at least two of the parts $S_i$ are non-empty.

*Proof.* If only one part, say $S_1$, were non-empty, then $V\setminus\{a\}=S_1$, and by Claim 1 every edge of the whole graph has colour $\alpha_1$ — contradicting the existence of two edges with distinct colours. ∎

So pick indices $i\neq j$ with $S_i,S_j$ both non-empty.

**Claim 3.** Every part $S_i$ is finite.

*Proof.* Fix a part $S_i$ and pick a vertex $v\in S_j$ for some $j\neq i$ (possible by Claim 2). We show the map $u\mapsto c_{uv}$ is injective on $S_i$.

First, $c_{uv}\neq\alpha_i$: if $c_{uv}=\alpha_i=c_{ua}$, then $(\ast)$ applied at $u$ to the pair $(a,v)$ gives $c_{av}=\alpha_i$, contradicting $c_{av}=\alpha_j\neq\alpha_i$. Similarly $c_{uv}\neq\alpha_j$ (apply $(\ast)$ at $v$ to $(a,u)$).

Now let $u_1,u_2\in S_i$ be distinct and suppose $c_{u_1 v}=c_{u_2 v}$. Applying $(\ast)$ at $v$ to the pair $(u_1,u_2)$ yields $c_{u_1 u_2}=c_{u_1 v}$. But $c_{u_1 u_2}=\alpha_i$ by Claim 1, so $c_{u_1 v}=\alpha_i$, contradicting the previous paragraph. Hence $c_{u_1 v}\neq c_{u_2 v}$; the colours $\{c_{uv}:u\in S_i\}$ are pairwise distinct.

All these colours are incident with $v$, and $v$ sees only finitely many colours. Therefore $|S_i|$ is finite. ∎

**Conclusion.** $V\setminus\{a\}=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}S_i$ is a finite union ( $k$ finite) of finite sets (Claim 3), hence finite. But $V$ is infinite — contradiction.

The contradiction refutes assumption $(\ast)$. Therefore there exist pairwise-distinct $x,y,z\in V$ with $c_{xy}=c_{xz}\neq c_{yz}$. $\blacksquare$

